WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.400
- Most architecture students
at most universities

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.750
learn to design buildings,

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.330
but don't actually build
anything bigger than a model.

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.310
Construction isn't an
architect's job, after all.

00:00:08.310 --> 00:00:09.870
Their job is to design.

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:12.690
But behind me is Poly Canyon,
up in the hills above

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.970
California Polytechnic State University,

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:16.590
or Cal Poly for short.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.990
For decades, some of the
senior architecture students

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:20.850
at Cal Poly have designed and built

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.580
experimental final projects at full scale

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:25.620
in the "architecture laboratory".

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.680
And some of those
projects are really weird.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.170
(in person) So everything here was
built by the students?

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:34.500
- Yes. A lot of times it's
based on their schoolwork

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.490
and it's in a topic that they
want to explore more deeply.

00:00:38.490 --> 00:00:41.130
They come up with the design,

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.960
the materials that they need,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:44.820
they do a cost estimate.

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:48.210
Some of these go back...
I'm not quite sure if the '60s,

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.040
but definitely early '70s.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.840
- So how many structures are there?

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.310
- I think there's about 21 or 22
that are still kind-of standing.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:00:59.490
- It's not like every student does
a senior project that's here?

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:01.710
It's, if they've got a design
they want to make real,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.090
and they can find the funding for it?

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:03.923
- Exactly.

00:01:03.923 --> 00:01:05.430
- Cal Poly will give
them the space to do it.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:06.263
- Exactly.

00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:08.040
- How often is there a new building here?

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.210
- They come really in spurts.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:10.980
Like someone will build and I think then

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.600
the students hear about
other groups building,

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:14.640
so then they might try and do something.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.230
We might get one project maybe
every two to three years, on average.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.050
They try and make it like you
were working in the real world.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.930
So you have to go through our
facilities department here

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.880
and they have to get a building permit.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.420
And these days there's also a plan for
what will be the upkeep.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:33.300
So it's open to public.
So if you're on campus,

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.480
everyone is welcome to
explore the area.

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.970
And I was amazed when I first
started coming out here again,

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.630
'cause I went to Cal Poly as an undergrad
and so when I came back,

00:01:42.630 --> 00:01:45.930
I was really amazed at how
much the Canyon gets used.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:48.330
And especially in the
afternoons and on weekends.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.800
There's quite a bit of foot traffic.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:54.240
- I've seen little tensegrity structures
for, you know, desk toys.

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:56.580
But that's a full building.

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:57.870
That's, that's massive!

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.420
That must have been done in
computer simulation first.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.860
- They did do some computer modeling.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.420
I had them build some
models with stick and string

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.220
so that we could just make
sure we got the geometry right.

00:02:08.220 --> 00:02:09.780
- Close up, this almost seems reasonable.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:11.310
My brain's not breaking, looking at it.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.210
And then you take a step back

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.910
and you realize that this
whole central section

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:16.770
and the two things dangling down here,

00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:19.470
it's just these cables.

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:20.303
- Exactly, yeah.

00:02:20.303 --> 00:02:22.350
- It really feels like
it should drop down.

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:23.850
When the buildings here are completed,

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.160
they're left to stand,
to face the elements,

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.160
which means some locals call this place
the "architecture graveyard".

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:30.510
But I don't think that's fair,

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:31.980
because there is upkeep here.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.780
The buildings are maintained,

00:02:33.780 --> 00:02:36.900
but cost and time are
always going to be issues.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.240
- There was a time when
people used to live here.

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.073
- Really?

00:02:40.073 --> 00:02:42.510
- They were curators of Poly Canyon.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.820
And so they lived in some
of these various structures.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.301
They would stay here.

00:02:46.301 --> 00:02:48.257
And they had running water and
some electricity,  

00:02:48.257 --> 00:02:50.368
but they would stay here
and the idea was that they

00:02:50.368 --> 00:02:54.090
would do minor repairs
in exchange for housing.

00:02:54.090 --> 00:02:55.470
It was a really interesting time.

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:59.130
This was the last space that
was used for caretakers.

00:02:59.130 --> 00:03:01.870
Two bedrooms. They were in--
- Wow.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.570
- One was in this module here,

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.790
this square that you see above your head.

00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:07.230
And then there was another bedroom

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:09.210
right over this module here.

00:03:09.210 --> 00:03:11.400
And then they had a kitchen.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:13.830
A bathroom was right here.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.950
And then like a living
space was right over here.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:18.900
But everything's stable.
It holds up.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:19.889
- Yeah.

00:03:19.889 --> 00:03:21.240
- And you know, it's
mostly visitors like us,

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.670
so there's not a lot
of activity down here.

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.440
- Who funds it if they
want to build something?

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.480
- Right, so a lot of
times the college may have

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.190
a little bit of money to help them,

00:03:29.190 --> 00:03:31.350
more like seed money to get them started.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.440
But the students will go
out to companies that have

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:36.450
a relationship with the college

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.210
and they'll pretty much ask
for, not necessarily money,

00:03:39.210 --> 00:03:42.090
but materials that that company sells.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:44.790
- And they're actually doing the graft
to put stuff together as well?

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:45.660
- They did everything together.

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.500
It's amazing what you'll see.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.653
And then actually two of them ended up
coming back and getting married here.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:52.313
- In Poly Canyon?

00:03:52.313 --> 00:03:53.880
- In Poly Canyon, under their structures.

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:55.110
- I really like this place.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:57.000
I've wanted to visit here for a long time,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:58.770
but parts of it are run down.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:01.980
As soon as one vandal tags a
structure and breaks the ice,

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.690
a lot more people think it's somehow okay

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.070
to turn up with spray paint.

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.420
I'm worried about publicizing this.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:08.220
I've got a responsibility
to the places I visit

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:09.300
and the people I work with.

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.010
And there are plenty of other things

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:13.380
that have been ruined
by too many tourists...

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:14.880
...or too many YouTubers.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:16.770
But Poly Canyon is open to the public.

00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:19.620
You can hike out here and stand
in these buildings yourself.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:21.750
This isn't an architecture graveyard,

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.250
it's an art gallery.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.780
- The hope was, you know, if we can show
that it is still living, right?

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:30.090
Then maybe people start to take care of it

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:33.150
so that it can last for the
next generation of students.

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.350
- See, now I want to come down here

00:04:34.350 --> 00:04:35.520
with a bucket of whitewash and help.

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:37.290
- Exactly, yes.
Well, I will let you know,

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:38.300
if you happen to be in California...!

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:40.620
- I might take you up on that.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:41.685
- Or even if you know people.

00:04:41.685 --> 00:04:42.810
- Yeah.

00:04:42.810 --> 00:04:43.968
- Yeah, we'll put you up for the night.

